  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "On the asymptotic expansions of the Kashaev invariant of the knots with 6 crossings — We give presentations of the asymptotic expansions of the Kashaev invariant of the knots with 6 crossings.\n\n**On the asymptotic expansions of the Kashaev invariant of the knots with 6 crossings**\n\n*Tomotada Ohtsuki*\n\n72 pp, 28 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We give presentations of the asymptotic expansions of the Kashaev invariant of the knots with 6 crossings. In particular, we show the volume conjecture for these knots, which states that the leading terms of the expansions present the hyperbolic volume and the Chern-Simons invariant of the complements of the knots. As higher coeﬃcients of the expansions, we obtain a new series of invariants of these knots. A non-trivial part of the proof is to apply the saddle point method to calculate the asymptotic expansion of an integral which presents the Kashaev invariant. A key step of this part is to give a concrete homotopy of the (real 3-dimensional) domain of the integral in C3 in such a way that the boundary of the domain always stays in a certain domain in C3 given by the potential function of the hyperbolic structure. Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation (2010). Primary: 57M27.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 2 Preliminaries; 2.2 Some behaviors of the dilogarithm function; 2.3 Deﬁnition of the Kashaev invariant; 2.4 The Poisson summation formula; 2.5 The saddle point method; 3.1 Proof of Theorem 1.1 for the 61 knot; 3.2 Estimate of the range of ∆′; 3.3 Calculation of the critical value; 3.4 Verifying the assumption of the Poisson su; 3.5 Verifying the assumption of the saddle poi; 4.1 Proof of Theorem 1.1 for the 62 knot; 4.2 Estimate of the range of ∆′; 4.3 Calculation of the critical value; 4.4 Verifying the assumption of the Poisson su; 4.5 Verifying the assumption of the saddle poi; 5.1 Proof of Theorem 1.1 for the 63 knot",
